Agreed on the defect, fixed at the boundary rather than around `removeSupersededCompany` — and one premise of the report needs correcting.

The chain already had an unguarded `await` before this PR: the feature-flag read sits outside the try/catch that guards the vendor calls, and it did on the base branch too.

https://github.com/naboo-team/naboo/blob/b3877081c0837fa555aef17e4fab31dfa8016067/packages/wome-api/src/domain-modules/listing/house/sub-modules/house-partner-health/sub-modules/creditsafe-report/services/creditsafe-report.service.ts#L336-L342

So a try/catch around `removeSupersededCompany` would have left that one open and put a second guard where one suffices. The fix is a `.catch()` on the detached call itself, which is also how `typescript.md` spells the rule ("Every promise must have error handling. `fetchUser(id).catch(handleError)`"):

    void this.enrolInMonitoringPortfolio({ … }).catch((error: unknown) => {
      this.logger.error('partner-health.enrolment.detached-failed', error, {
        houseId,
        connectId,
      })
    })

That covers the new `distinct` and `matchIsStillClaimed` reads, the pre-existing flag read, and anything later added to the chain. Covered by a new case: "should log and swallow a read that rejects inside the detached chain".

Agreed, applied:

    type RemoveSupersededCompanyParams = EnrolInMonitoringPortfolioParams & {
      portfolioId: number
    }

Beyond the line count, the intersection states something true that the duplication left implicit: `removeSupersededCompany` is called from inside `enrolInMonitoringPortfolio` with exactly its parameters plus the portfolio it resolved, so the shapes are not merely alike — one is the other extended. A field added to the enrolment params now reaches the removal by construction rather than by someone remembering.

`typescript.md` lists `T & { extra: string }` among the derivation forms, so this is the spelling the rule asks for. Typecheck clean, 56 tests green on the service.

You are right, and the comment was the wrong half of a contradiction I wrote myself: `reconcile-portfolio.ts` already says "nothing here counts a company the portfolio holds that nothing selects any more", while this one claimed the reconciliation catches exactly that. Three things confirm your reading — the `Pick` is `'addPortfolioCompany' | 'listPortfolioCompanies'` with no removal at all, `driftCount` sums only missing-from-portfolio categories, and the ticket itself calls the blind spot structural: "This is the opposite direction — held by the portfolio but no longer selected."

Your traced path holds too. The add fails, the early return keeps A while row B is already written; the next refresh hits `supersededConnectId === connectId` and returns; A is never looked at again.

The comment now says what is true:

    // Swallowed, and nothing picks it up: the reconciliation only adds what
    // the portfolio lacks, so a company left behind stays billed until the
    // surplus diff ships.

On piece (2) itself, I am de-scoping it here rather than squeezing it in, and there is a concrete reason beyond size: #17655 deletes `scripts/enrol-monitoring-portfolio/reconcile-portfolio.ts` and folds it into `services/reconcile-portfolio.ts` on the cron. Writing the surplus diff in the file that another in-flight PR moves guarantees a conflict and lands the logic in the version that is going away. It belongs on top of #17655, where the reconciliation already reads the membership set every week.

No follow-up ticket opened yet — that is the author's call, not mine to make on a review thread. Flagging it here so the de-scope is explicit rather than silent, which was your second option.

### An empty 204 body would count every successful removal as a failure

**Medium Severity**

This result schema is modelled on `addPortfolioCompanyResultSchema` — a POST that genuinely returns a body — rather than on the precedent nine lines up in this same file for a write verb that commonly answers 204:

```ts
export const putEventRulesResultSchema = z.preprocess(
  (value) => {
    // A 204 carries no body, yet the vendor also declares a `{ message }` payload on it.
    if (value === '') { return {} }
    return value
  },
  z.object({ message: z.string().nullish() }).loose(),
)
```

`BaseResource.request` ends with `schema.safeParse(response.data)` (`base.ts:113`), and axios hands back `''` for an empty body.

**Impact:** if CreditSafe answers the DELETE 204/empty, `safeParse` fails → `CreditsafeValidationError` → `throwUpstream('removePortfolioCompany', …)` → `CreditsafeUpstreamError` with `status: null`. `matchIsCompanyAbsent` is then false, so every *successful* removal is logged `partner-health.unenrolment.failed` and counted `naboo.partner_health.unenrolment{outcome:failure}` — a monitor on `outcome:failure` would be permanently red, which is the one thing the new metric was split three ways to avoid.

The new SDK spec only covers a 200-with-body, so nothing catches it.

**Fix:** wrap the result schema the same way `putEventRulesResultSchema` is wrapped, and add the empty-204 case next to the 200 case in `tests/monitoring.spec.ts`.

Caught a real one, and the chain you trace is exactly right — a 204 would have made every successful removal log `partner-health.unenrolment.failed` and count `outcome:failure`, permanently reddening the one signal the three-way split was meant to keep meaningful.

Wrapped the same way as the precedent you point at nine lines up:

    export const removePortfolioCompanyResultSchema = z.preprocess(
      (value) => {
        // A DELETE commonly answers 204 with no body, which axios hands back as ''.
        if (value === '') {
          return {}
        }
        return value
      },
      z.object({ correlationId: …, message: … }).loose(),
    )

Added `should resolve the removal when the vendor answers 204 with no body` to `tests/monitoring.spec.ts` next to the 200 case. I checked it fails against the unwrapped schema before fixing it, so the test earns its place.

### The `dogstatsd` spy leaks into the rest of the file when an assertion fails

**Medium Severity**

This test and the one at `:919` install `vi.spyOn(dogstatsd, 'increment').mockImplementation(...)` and call `incrementSpy.mockRestore()` as the **last statement of the test body**.

`vitest.legacy.config.ts:98` sets `clearMocks: true` (call history only) but not `restoreMocks`, and this file has no `afterEach` cleanup for the spy. These two tests are the first and only place in the file that patches `dogstatsd` — every other test, including the other six in this `describe`, leaves it as the real module singleton.

**Impact:** if any `expect(...)` inside either test throws before reaching `mockRestore()`, the no-op implementation stays installed on the shared module singleton for the remainder of the run, silently swallowing every later `dogstatsd.increment` — including in unrelated `describe` blocks further down the file. One genuine failure becomes a cascade of misleading green.

**Fix:** make the restore unconditional:

```ts
try {
  // assertions
} finally {
  incrementSpy.mockRestore()
}
```

or hoist the spy and its restore into a `beforeEach`/`afterEach` pair scoped to these two tests, so cleanup does not depend on the body succeeding.

Agreed, and the blast radius you describe is the part that makes it worth fixing: a no-op left on the shared `dogstatsd` singleton turns one real failure into a run of misleading green.

Took your second option, scoped to the describe that owns both tests:

    // `clearMocks` clears call history, never the implementation, so a spy on
    // the shared `dogstatsd` singleton outlives a test that throws mid-body.
    afterEach(() => {
      vi.restoreAllMocks()
    })

The two inline `mockRestore()` calls are gone. These are the only two `vi.spyOn` in the file, so nothing else is affected by the blanket restore.
